+faqClusterOpen
	+faqItem('Is what you apply safe around children and pets?', 'b0')
		p The product we use and apply is TGA-registered, and it's applied in line with the manufacturer's directions. We request the work area be restricted from household access while we work, and we'll tell you when it can be used again.
	+faqItem('What actually causes mould in a home?', 'b1')
		p Moisture. Almost without exception.
		p Spores are already in the air — in every home in the country, all year round. They only become your problem when they land somewhere with enough moisture to grow on. So the question is never really "where did the mould come from", it's "where is the water coming from".
		p The usual drivers we find:
		ul
			li #[strong A quiet leak.] Under a sink, behind a shower wall, a roof or gutter issue that only shows itself after heavy rain.
			li #[strong Condensation.] Warm humid air meeting cold surfaces with nowhere to go. Bathrooms without a working exhaust fan and bedrooms shut up over winter are the classics.
			li #[strong A one-off water event.] Burst pipe, storm, flood — where the surface looked dry within days but the material underneath wasn't.
			li #[strong Rising or lateral damp] through walls and slabs.
			li #[strong Dead air.] Wardrobes on external walls, furniture pushed flat against cold walls, rooms that never get opened.
		p It's why the first thing we look for is the water, not the growth. The growth is the symptom.
